statements.statement:
For every \(q\geq1\) and \(L\geq4\), there is a constant \(c_{\mathrm{CTY}}=c_{\mathrm{CTY}}(q,L)>0\) such that
\[
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}
 \left(\frac{n}{\log n}\right)^{1/4}
 \inf_{\widehat\mu_n\in\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{NP}}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_{\mathrm{NP}}(L,q)}
 \mathbb E_P\!\left[
 \sup_{x\in\operatorname{bd}(\mathcal X_P)}
 |\widehat\mu_n(x)-\mu_P(x)|
 \right]
 =\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{R_n^{\mathrm{NP}}}{a_n}
 \geq c_{\mathrm{CTY}}.
\]
Thus the logarithmic penalty is necessary in the coordinatewise distance decision class and on the same nonparametric law class used by Cattaneo, Titiunik, and Yu, Theorem~6; the result is not a subclass transfer from the two-sided-thickness treatment-effect model.

For every \(q\geq1\) and \(L\geq4\), there is a constant \(c_{\mathrm{PI}}=c_{\mathrm{PI}}(q,L)>0\) such that
\[
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}
 \left(\frac{n}{\log n}\right)^{1/4}
 \inf_{\widehat\mu_n^{\mathrm{PI}}\in\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{PI}}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_{\mathrm{NP}}(L,q)}
 \mathbb E_P^*\!\left[
 \sup_{x\in\operatorname{bd}(\mathcal X_P)}
 \left|\widehat\mu_{n,P}^{\mathrm{PI}}(x)-\mu_P(x)\right|
 \right]
 =\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{R_n^{\mathrm{PI}}}{a_n}
 \geq c_{\mathrm{PI}}.
\]
Here \(\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{PI}}\) permits an arbitrary law-independent family \(\{T_{n,x}:x\in\mathbb R^2\}\) whose fixed-\(x\) sections are Borel measurable; no joint measurability, continuity, or common modulus in \(x\) is imposed, and \(\mathbb E_P^*\) is outer expectation.  Thus the logarithmic penalty remains necessary in a decision class strictly larger than the inherited common-map Cattaneo--Titiunik--Yu class.  The theorem is a lower bound on the same nonparametric law class \(\mathcal P_{\mathrm{NP}}(L,q)\), not an Assumption~2 upper-bound result or a matched two-sided frontier.

estimand_functional:
For each \(x\in\operatorname{bd}(\mathcal X_P)\), the observed input is \(V_{n,P}(x)=((Y_i,\lVert X_i-x\rVert_2):1\leq i\leq n)\).  The enlarged point-indexed class \(\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{PI}}\) consists of all law-independent families \(\{T_{n,x}:x\in\mathbb R^2\}\) for which every fixed-\(x\) section is Borel measurable and \(\widehat\mu_{n,P}^{\mathrm{PI}}(x)=T_{n,x}(V_{n,P}(x))\).  No joint measurability or other regularity in \(x\) is imposed.  Its explicit minimax functional is
\[
 R_n^{\mathrm{PI}}
 =\inf_{\widehat\mu_n^{\mathrm{PI}}\in\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{PI}}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_{\mathrm{NP}}(L,q)}
 \mathbb E_P^*\!\left[
 \sup_{x\in\operatorname{bd}(\mathcal X_P)}
 \left|\widehat\mu_{n,P}^{\mathrm{PI}}(x)-\mu_P(x)\right|
 \right],
\]
where \(\mathbb E_P^*\) is outer expectation.  The inherited class \(\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{NP}}\) is the strict subclass obtained by requiring one common Borel map \(T_n\).  For a common map, the sample-point loss is jointly Borel in the sample and \(x\); its supremum over the compact boundary is universally measurable, and its ordinary completed expectation equals its outer expectation.  Consequently \(R_n^{\mathrm{NP}}\geq R_n^{\mathrm{PI}}\).  The point-indexed theorem proves \(R_n^{\mathrm{PI}}\gtrsim(\log n/n)^{1/4}\), and class inclusion gives the CTY common-map converse.  The published uniform upper theorem imposes its additional Assumption~2 geometric condition, so no full-\(\mathcal P_{\mathrm{NP}}(L,q)\) upper bound or matched two-sided frontier is claimed.
